Given a string s of digits and an integer k, remove k digits from s to form the smallest possible number while keeping the order of the remaining digits. The resulting string should not have leading zeros and if it becomes empty, return "0".Examples: Input: s = "4325043", k = 3Output: "2043"Explanation: By removing three digits (4, 3, 5) while preserving the order of the remaining digits, we obtain "2043", which is the smallest possible number.Input: s = "765028321", k = 5Output: "221"Explanation: Removing five digits (7, 6, 5, 8, 3) results in "0221". Since the number should not contain leading zeros, the final answer is "221".Try It YourselfTable of Content[Naive Approach] Iterative Greedy Removal - O(n*k) Time and O(n) Space[Better Approach] Using Stack - O(n) Time and O(n) Space[Expected Approach] In-Place Digit Removal - O(n) Time and O(1) Space[Naive Approach] Iterative Greedy Removal - O(n*k) Time and O(n) SpaceWe run two nested loop where the outer loops runs through the current string and the inner loop removes the first digit that is greater than its next digit, since removing a larger digit earlier leads to a smaller number. If no such digit exists, remove the last digit.
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

string removeKdig(string s, int k) {
    while (k--) {
        int i = 0;

        // Find first digit greater than next
        while (i < s.size() - 1 && s[i] <= s[i + 1]) {
            i++;
        }

        // Remove that digit
        s.erase(i, 1);
    }

    // Remove leading zeros
    int i = 0;
    while (i < s.size() && s[i] == '0') i++;

    s = s.substr(i);

    return s.empty() ? "0" : s;
}

int main() {
    string s = "765028321";
    int k = 5;
    cout << removeKdig(s, k);
    return 0;
}


